  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nUpper level ridge across the southwestern US is expected to remain \nthrough the period.  Broad upper level trough across the Dakotas is \nexpected to intensify and surface cold front is expected to build \nsouth through the region tomorrow.  Ahead of the front, a weakly \ncapped atmosphere could lead to periodic rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms across the region.  \n\nAs the low level jet develops this evening across the Central \nPlains, there is the potential for elevated non severe storms to \ndevelop across eastern KS building east into western and central \nMissouri.  With precipitable water values of nearly 2.3\" and warm \ncloud depths of 13-14K feet, anything that develops would be very \nefficient in producing rainfall.  Individual storms are expected to \nbuild east 15-20 knots, which should be enough movement to prevent \nflooding unless there is training of storms.   \n\nThunderstorms are expected to develop near the low center this \nevening across south central South Dakota into northern Nebraska. \nThese storms are expected to organize in time and build down the \nthermal ridge/ instability axis potentially impacting the region \nafter 06Z tonight. 0-6km bulk shear is expected to generally be 20-\n25 knots, so storms may struggle a bit to organize into a MCS; \nhowever, storms will be building southeast into an increasing low \nlevel jet that may aid in an isolated wind threat.  \n\nThese storms are expected to outrun the surface front- creating a \nconditional threat for the potential for redeveloping storms on \nFriday with sufficient destabilization. SPC has upgraded the day 2 to \nslight risk across portions of central into eastern Missouri for the \npotential of gusty winds and a conditional tornado threat if storms \nare able to become surface based. \n\nBreezy northwest winds are expected to develop Friday night and \ncontinue into Saturday with 30-40 knots of flow at 925 hpa. 850 hpa \ntemperatures are expected to fall into the lower teens, keeping high \ntemperatures unseasonably cool on Saturday.  \n\nTemperatures are expected to gradually warm next week towards \nclimatological norms.  Precipitation chances return to the region \nlate week as additional waves build east along the periphery of the \nupper ridge.",
